"""AQS-SAF-005 fire protection and detection (X-SAF-05, A-M-188; rev B X-FIR-01: sprinkler zone map,
pipe schedule class, fire-water tank and fire pump as drivers, graph fire params). Sprinkler demand
per level and zone (flow_graph/param/fire.py), water supplies, detection, special hazards, and the
API 521 fire case of the gas receivers carried from X-SAF-04.
Run: python3 docs-authority/_fire.py           (doc only, as the pipeline stage)
     python3 docs-authority/_fire.py --write   (also stamps params.fire on ROOF_TANK / CATCH_TANK; flock the repo design lock)"""
import json, math, os, sys
R = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))); os.chdir(R); sys.path.insert(0, R); sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(R, 'models/freecad'))
import params as P
from flow_graph.param import fire as F
from flow_graph.param import receivers as RC, pressure_nodes as PN, drivers as DRp, site as SITE
from flow_graph.param.sizing import relief as RL
from flow_graph.param.refs import cite, REFS
_ORDER = []
def ref(k):
    """Number a reference in the order it is first cited (DOC_STYLE) and return its marker."""
    cite(k)
    if k not in _ORDER: _ORDER.append(k)
    return f'[{_ORDER.index(k) + 1}]'
g = json.load(open('flow_graph/plant_flow_graph.json')); N = {n['unit_id']: n for n in g['nodes']}
rows, Vf = F.demand(P.PLATE_L * P.PLATE_W / 1e6); H = P.HYDRO
zs = F.zones()
sup = F.supply(rows, Vf, SITE.g() * H['z_res0_m'])
_d = DRp.load()
gv = max(rows, key=lambda r: r['V_m3'])
bore = math.sqrt(4 * sup['demand_Lmin'] / 60000 / math.pi / 6) * 1000
md = ['# Fire protection and detection', '', 'Document number: AQS-SAF-005', 'Revision: C', 'Date: 2026-10-29',
      'Generated by `docs-authority/_fire.py` from `flow_graph/param/fire.py`, the plant graph and the `fire_*` keys of `flow_graph/param/drivers.json`. Do not edit by hand; change the generator or its inputs and run `python3 -m flow_graph.param.regen`.',
      'Authority: AQS-PLAN-007 tasks X-SAF-05 and X-FIR-01.', '',
      '| Rev | Description | Date |', '| --- | --- | --- |',
      '| A | First issue: sprinkler demand per level, two water supplies, detection and the fire case of the gas receivers (A-M-188) | 2026-10-16 |',
      '| B | Hazard zones, pipe class, fire-water tank and fire pump sized from the drivers; graph fire parameters written (X-FIR-01) | 2026-10-20 |',
      '| C | Rewritten to DOC_STYLE: purpose and scope added, the two descriptions of the water supplies merged into one section, tables numbered and references listed | 2026-10-29 |', '',
      '## 1. Purpose and scope', '',
      'This page states the fire protection of the plant: the sprinkler demand of each level and hazard zone, the sprinkler piping, the two fire-water supplies, the fire detection, and the fire case of the high-pressure gas receivers. It is a study. The fire engineer of record confirms the hazard classes and the hydraulic calculation.',
      f'The hazard classes are the occupancy classes of NFPA 13 {ref("NFPA13_2025")}: light hazard (LH), ordinary hazard group 1 and group 2 (OH1, OH2) and extra hazard group 1 (EH1). The lower flammability limit is abbreviated LFL.', '',
      '## 2. Sprinkler demand per level', '',
      f'Table 2.1 gives the sprinkler demand of each level. The sprinkler flow is the design density times the design area, plus 10 % for the imbalance of a hydraulically designed system. The hose allowance and the duration come from NFPA 13 Table 19.2.3.1.1 {ref("NFPA13_2025")}.', '',
      'Table 2.1. Sprinkler demand per level', '',
      '| Level | Occupancy | Hazard class | Density (mm/min) | Design area (m²) | Sprinkler flow (L/min) | Hose allowance (L/min) | Duration (min) | Volume (m³) |', '|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|']
md += [f"| {r['level']} | {r['occ']} | {r['hazard']} | {r['density']} | {r['area']:.0f} | {r['sprink_Lmin']:.0f} | {r['hose_Lmin']:.0f} | {r['dur_min']:.0f} | {r['V_m3']:.0f} |" for r in rows]
md += ['', f"Level {gv['level']} governs. Its class is {gv['hazard']}, and it needs {gv['sprink_Lmin'] + gv['hose_Lmin']:.0f} L/min for {gv['dur_min']:.0f} min, which is {Vf:.0f} m³ of water.", '',
       '## 3. Hazard zones', '',
       'A level can contain zones whose hazard differs from the level as a whole. The zones follow the hazardous-area release sources of AQS-ELEC-009 and the hazard separation layout of AQS-SAF-007. Table 3.1 gives the class, system and demand of each zone. The classes and densities come from the driver keys `fire_zone_hazard` and `fire_nfpa13_hazards`, which follow the density curves of NFPA 13 chapter 19 ' + ref('NFPA13_2025') + '. The classification stays provisional until the owner issues the programme for the hall (X-FIR-01).', '',
       'Table 3.1. Sprinkler demand per hazard zone', '',
       '| Zone | Level | Hazard class | System | Density (mm/min) | Design area (m²) | Sprinkler flow (L/min) | Hose allowance (L/min) | Duration (min) |', '|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|']
md += [f"| {z['zone']} | {z['level']} | {z['hazard']} | {z['system']} | {z['density']} | {z['area']:.0f} | {z['sprink_Lmin']:.0f} | {z['hose_Lmin']:.0f} | {z['dur_min']:.0f} |" for z in zs]
md += ['', f"No zone needs more than the governing level, so the design demand is {sup['demand_Lmin']} L/min including the hose allowance.", '',
       '## 4. Sprinkler piping', '',
       f"The sprinkler pipe is Schedule 40 black steel to ASTM A53. The system is hydraulically designed by the density and area method. The pipe schedule method of NFPA 13 chapter 7 {ref('NFPA13_2025')} is not used, because it does not cover extra hazard occupancies and level L0 contains EH1 zones.",
       f"The riser and feed mains carry the design demand of {sup['demand_Lmin']} L/min at a velocity of 6 m/s. This needs a bore of {bore:.0f} mm, so the riser is DN{math.ceil(bore / 50) * 50}. Each branch main is sized for its zone demand at the same velocity.",
       f"The fire engineer of record makes the hydraulic calculation from the riser to the hydraulically most remote area. The fire pump head includes a design margin of {DRp.val(_d, 'fire_pump_head_margin_kPa')} kPa (driver `fire_pump_head_margin_kPa`) to cover it.", '',
       '## 5. Water supplies', '',
       f"The plant has two independent fire-water supplies, as the redundancy rule requires. Each can deliver the design demand, so a fire after a hydro discharge, or with one supply out of service, still has a full supply.", '',
       '### 5.1 Gravity supply', '',
       f"The gravity supply is the primary supply. The fire reserve is {sup['governing_V_m3']:.0f} m³, held as dead storage at the bottom of the water tower below the hydro outlet nozzles, so a hydro discharge cannot draw it {ref('NFPA22_2023')}. The reserve is split across the tower cells and each cell has its own fire outlet, so one cell drained for inspection leaves the others in service.",
       f"The static head is {H['z_res0_m']:.1f} m ({SITE.g() * H['z_res0_m']:.0f} kPa) at level L0 and {H['z_res0_m'] - (P.Z_L2 - P.CLEAR_L1) / 1000:.1f} m at the top floor of the habitation. The reserve is refilled within {sup['refill_h']} h (driver `fire_tank_refill_h`).", '',
       '### 5.2 Pumped supply', '',
       f"The fire pump is a listed electric horizontal split-case pump to NFPA 20 {ref('NFPA20_2025')}. It is fed from the critical bus, with the backup generator as its second supply. It draws from the lower reservoir `CATCH_TANK`, which holds {H['catch_depth_m'] * H['area_m2']:.0f} m³ when full and also collects the sprinkler run-off. The lower reservoir is sized for the hydro volume plus the fire reserve (AQS-BLD-014). The pump boosts the gravity supply where the hydraulics need more than the static head.",
       f"The pump is rated {sup['rated_Lmin']} L/min, the next listed size above the design demand of {sup['demand_Lmin']} L/min, at {sup['rated_head_kPa']} kPa. That head is the shortfall of the static head plus the design margin. The shut-off pressure is at most {sup['churn_max_kPa']} kPa ({DRp.val(_d, 'fire_pump_churn_frac'):.2f} times rated, NFPA 20 4.16). At 150 % of rated flow, {sup['runout_Lmin']} L/min, the pump delivers at least {sup['runout_head_min_kPa']} kPa, which is 65 % of rated head (NFPA 20 4.18). The motor draws about {sup['pump_kW']} kW at this head because the gravity supply provides most of it. A jockey pump holds the system pressure, and the pump is run weekly at shut-off as NFPA 25 {ref('NFPA25_2026')} requires.", '',
       '## 6. Detection and special hazards', '',
       f'Table 6.1 gives the detection and suppression for each area. Detection follows NFPA 72 {ref("NFPA72_2025")}.', '',
       'Table 6.1. Detection and suppression by area', '',
       '| Area | Detection | Suppression |', '|---|---|---|',
       '| Habitation and farm | Smoke detectors at NFPA 72 spacing; smoke alarms in the sleeping rooms | Wet-pipe sprinklers |',
       '| L0 shops, pyrolysis and oils | Rate-of-rise heat detectors, and flame detectors at the pyrolysis reactor | Wet-pipe sprinklers, EH1 |',
       '| Electrical rooms, rectifiers and inverters | Air-sampling smoke detection | Pre-action sprinklers, which hold no water in the pipes until a detector operates |',
       f'| Flow battery `ZNBR2` | Smoke detection and hydrogen detection for the enclosure purge {ref("NFPA855_2026")} | Wet-pipe sprinklers; the electrolyte is aqueous and does not burn |',
       f'| Hydrogen receiver `CRYO_H2` | Ultraviolet and infrared flame detectors, and hydrogen detectors that alarm at 10 % and trip at 25 % of the LFL {ref("NFPA2_2026")} | Water spray at 10.2 L/min per m² on the tube bank to cool it {ref("NFPA15_2027")}; automatic depressuring on flame detection |',
       '| Oxygen receiver `CRYO_O2` | Oxygen enrichment detector that alarms at 23.5 % oxygen, and flame detectors | Water spray as for hydrogen; oxygen-clean construction to ASTM G93 |',
       '| Carbon dioxide store `CRYO_CO2` | Carbon dioxide detector that alarms at 5,000 ppm | Water spray |',
       '| Cold box and L-1 wet process | Heat detectors | Wet-pipe sprinklers, OH2 |', '',
       '## 7. Fire case of the gas receivers', '',
       f"The relief valves of the gas receivers are sized for a fire under API 521 {ref('API521_2020')}. For tubes filled with gas the required area is A = F' A'/√P1, with F' = 0.1406/(C Kd) (Tw − T1)^1.25 / T1^0.6625 in US customary units, a wall temperature Tw of 1100 °F (593 °C) and P1 at 121 % of the set pressure. For the liquid carbon dioxide the heat input is Q = 21,000 F A^0.82 Btu/h on the wetted tube area, with F = 1 because drainage and fire-fighting are present; the liquid is relieved as vapour. Table 7.1 gives the results. The blocked-outlet bore is the relief case without fire, for comparison.", '',
       'Table 7.1. Fire case of the gas receivers', '',
       '| Receiver | Tubes | Exposed area (m²) | P1 (kPa abs) | T1 (K) | Fire area per half bank (mm²) | Fire bore per half bank (mm) | Blocked-outlet bore (mm) | Installed bore, two valves (mm) | Depressuring release (kg) | Depressuring bore (mm) |', '|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|']
_dp = DRp.load(); _mg = DRp.val(_dp, 'press_design_margin'); _win = DRp.val(_dp, 'ely_window_h')
_RR = RC.design(_dp, g)   # R11 Rev AC regen: the receiver design/relief params were stripped from the Rev AC graph; rebuilt in memory from the same sizing modules, no graph write
for u in F.GAS_FIRE:
    c = PN.GAS[u]; p = N[u]['params']; des = c['P_bar'] * _mg * 100
    W = sum((e['rate']['value'] or 0) / (_win if e['from_unit_id'] == 'H2O_ELYZ' else 24) for e in g['edges'] if e['to_unit_id'] == u and e['presence'] == 'present' and (e['rate'] or {}).get('unit') == 'kg/d')
    P1 = 1.10 * des + SITE.p_process_kPa()
    p['design'] = _RR[u]; p['design_kPa'] = round(des, 1); p.setdefault('relief_mm', 3.3)
    p['relief_basis'] = {'P1_kPa_abs': round(P1, 1), 'T_K': c['T_K'], 'M_kg_kmol': c['M'], 'k': c['k'], 'Z': c['Z'], 'W_kg_h': round(W, 4),
                         'bore_req_mm': round(RL.equiv_bore_mm(RL.gas_area_in2(W * 2.20462, c['k'], P1 / 6.894757, c['T_K'] * 1.8, c['Z'], c['M'])), 2)}
for u in F.GAS_FIRE:
    p = N[u]['params']; ds = p['design']; fc = p['relief_basis'].get('fire_case') or F.receiver_fire(p)
    p['relief_mm'] = float(math.ceil(max(p['relief_basis']['bore_req_mm'], fc['bore_req_mm_per_half'])))   # governing of blocked outlet and fire case per half bank (as the write_graph relief stage propagates)
    md.append(f"| {u} | {ds['tubes']} | {fc['exposed_m2']:.1f} | {fc['P1_kPa_abs']:.0f} | {fc['T1_K']:.0f} | {fc['A_req_mm2_per_half']:.1f} | {fc['bore_req_mm_per_half']:.1f} | {p['relief_basis']['bore_req_mm']} | {p['relief_mm']:.0f} | {fc['depress_kg']:.1f} | {fc['depress_bore_mm']:.2f} |")
md += ['', "The fire case governs every receiver. Each manifolded half bank has its own relief valve sized for a fire on its tubes, and the installed bore is written to the graph field `relief_mm` and to the relief register.",
       f"For tubes filled with gas, API 521 {ref('API521_2020')} warns that a relief valve alone may not prevent rupture in a fire, because the wall that no liquid wets heats up and loses strength while the pressure stays near the set pressure. The hydrogen and oxygen banks therefore depressure automatically on flame detection to 50 % of the design gauge pressure within 15 min (API 521 5.20, for a wall of 13.5 mm). The depressuring bore in Table 7.1 is sized at twice the average rate to cover the initial peak. Water spray keeps the tubes cool {ref('NFPA15_2027')}. The depressuring vents discharge to the same vent stacks as the relief valves.", '',
       '## 8. Open items', '',
       'The fire engineer of record must make the hydraulic calculation from the riser to the hydraulically most remote sprinkler, confirm the hazard classes when the owner issues the programme for the hall, and locate the fire department connection on the site.', '',
       '## References', '']
md += [f'{i + 1}. {REFS[k]}' for i, k in enumerate(_ORDER)]
open('docs-authority/FIRE_PROTECTION.md', 'w').write('\n'.join(md) + '\n')

if '--write' in sys.argv:   # X-FIR-01: stamp the graph fire params (run under the repo design lock)
    for u, role in (('ROOF_TANK', 'gravity supply: fire reserve as water-tower dead storage'),
                    ('CATCH_TANK', 'pumped supply: fire-pump suction source and sprinkler run-off')):
        p = N[u].setdefault('params', {})
        p['fire'] = {'role': role, 'governing_level': sup['governing_level'], 'governing_hazard': sup['governing_hazard'],
                     'reserve_m3': round(Vf, 1), 'demand_Lmin': sup['demand_Lmin'], 'duration_min': gv['dur_min'],
                     'pump_rated_Lmin': sup['rated_Lmin'], 'pump_rated_head_kPa': sup['rated_head_kPa'],
                     'pump_kW': sup['pump_kW'], 'refill_h': sup['refill_h'],
                     'basis': f'AQS-SAF-005 rev B (X-FIR-01); drivers fire_* keys; NFPA13_2025 / NFPA20_2025 / NFPA22_2023'}
    open('flow_graph/plant_flow_graph.json', 'w').write(json.dumps(g, indent=2))
    print('graph fire params stamped on ROOF_TANK, CATCH_TANK')
print(round(Vf), gv['level'], '| zones', len(zs), '| pump', sup['rated_Lmin'], 'L/min at', sup['rated_head_kPa'], 'kPa')
